/* The handoff's hero band: a white-to-lilac gradient panel at radius 26,
 * with a large dashed ring and a soft violet pool behind whatever the screen
 * puts in it. Six screens in the bundle draw this surface -
 * `Onboarding.dc.html:43`, `Gene Ark:47`, `Gene Lab:51`,
 * `Splice Chamber:101`, `Splice Reveal:43` and `Hybrid Growth:48` - and
 * three of those six draw the ring inside it.
 *
 * THE ROOT IS THE SHADOW WRAPPER, NOT THE BAND, exactly as `SectionCard`'s
 * is. Theme.uss's header note 2: UI Toolkit clips a background image to the
 * element's own box, so a nine-sliced drop shadow has to be drawn by
 * something LARGER than the surface it falls around. `.elev-2` goes on this
 * element - padding plus the shadow sprite and nothing else - and the band's
 * own fill lives on `.hero-band__surface` inside it.
 *
 * .elev-2 AND NOT .elev-1, WHICH IS THE HANDOFF BEING EXPLICIT. Every one of
 * the six draws the band at `0 4px 16px rgba(63,58,82,.08)` while the cards
 * above and below it are `0 2px 8px rgba(63,58,82,.06)` - the 2:1 pair
 * Tokens.uss's elevation block already maps to the two classes. The band
 * sits a step above the cards around it and the handoff says so twice, in
 * the offset and in the blur.
 *
 * GROWTH IS `HeroBand.Fill`'s JOB AND IT IS NOT A DECLARATION IN THIS FILE.
 * That is the whole lesson of Task 14b and it is worth the sentence:
 * `flex-grow` on an elevation WRAPPER stretches the wrapper while the
 * surface keeps its own height, so the nine-slice draws its drop around bare
 * paper - measured at 160px of shadow-on-nothing below the founder screen's
 * hero, seen by no test and caught only by the capture. The note is at
 * `SectionCard.uss` beside `.section-card__surface`. Here the two halves
 * cannot come apart: `Fill` writes `flex-grow` on BOTH elements from one
 * method, and `AHeroBandThatFillsGrowsItsSurfaceAndNotOnlyItsWrapper` reads
 * both. A screen never writes either one.
 */
.hero-band {
    flex-direction: column;
    flex-shrink: 0;
}

/* THE GRADIENT IS A TEXTURE BECAUSE USS HAS NO `linear-gradient`. Theme.uss's
 * header item 1; band-ramp.png is white to --violet-tint, stretched, and
 * generate-textures.py's ramp block has why it is not the hybrid ramp even
 * though the two are the same handoff declaration.
 *
 * THE COLOUR UNDER THE IMAGE IS --violet-tint AND IT IS THE RAMP'S OWN DEEP
 * END, on `.panel-hybrid`'s precedent: nothing else is under it, so the fill
 * costs nothing while the texture is present and is what the band degrades
 * to if the texture ever fails to import. This phase exists partly because a
 * missing asset rendered as nothing and nobody noticed.
 *
 * `overflow: hidden` IS THE HANDOFF'S OWN, on five of the six instances, and
 * it is load-bearing rather than tidy: the ring is 264px in a band that can
 * be shorter than that, and a rounded surface does not clip its children to
 * the radius without it - so without this the ring's dashes would be drawn
 * outside the band's corners. */
.hero-band__surface {
    flex-direction: column;
    border-radius: var(--radius-band);
    background-image: url("project:///Assets/UI/Art/band-ramp.png");
    -unity-background-scale-mode: stretch-to-fill;
    background-color: var(--violet-tint);
    overflow: hidden;
}

/* THE TINT HOOK, AND IT SWAPS A RAMP RATHER THAN A COLOUR. Phase 9 Task 18.
 * `HeroBand.Tint`'s own comment has the measurement in full; the short form is
 * that the five other-tinted bands in the bundle do NOT run white-to-tint the
 * way the violet six do - `Wave Defeat.dc.html:31` runs `#fbeee9 -> #f6e2e4`
 * - so multiplying this file's own ramp through
 * `-unity-background-image-tint-color` reproduces coral to 15 in summed channel
 * distance and the four blue-grey ones to 17 WITH THE SIGN OF `G - R`
 * REVERSED, which is the violet ramp's cast coming through a wash. Each tint
 * is therefore its own two-stop texture at the handoff's own endpoints, which
 * is what every other ramp in `generate-textures.py` already is.
 *
 * TWO CLASSES, AND THE ROOT CARRIES THE MODIFIER. `.hero-band--coral` goes on
 * the band (HeroBand's constructor) and this reaches DOWN to the surface
 * inside it - the direction StatCell.uss's closing note allows. Two classes
 * also beats the one-class base rule above however the panel orders the sheet,
 * which is the rule every override in this phase follows.
 *
 * BOTH DECLARATIONS MOVE TOGETHER OR NEITHER DOES. The base rule's own note
 * three paragraphs up says the fill under the image IS the ramp's deep end and
 * is what the band degrades to when a texture fails to import; a tint that
 * changed the image and left `--violet-tint` underneath would degrade to the
 * WRONG band, silently, in exactly the case nobody is looking. */
.hero-band--coral .hero-band__surface {
    background-image: url("project:///Assets/UI/Art/band-ramp-coral.png");
    background-color: var(--coral-tint-deep);
}

/* A full-bleed layer whose only job is to centre the ring, because USS has
 * no way to say "65% of my parent's width, square". There is no `calc()` and
 * no `aspect-ratio` on 6000.6.0f1, so a percentage width cannot produce a
 * matching height and the ring has to be a fixed square - which then needs
 * something to centre it inside a band of unknown size. This is that
 * something.
 *
 * ABSOLUTE, SO THE SUBJECT'S LAYOUT NEVER SEES IT. The handoff draws the
 * ring as an `<svg style="position: absolute; inset: 0">` for the same
 * reason: it is behind the content, not above it in the column. */
.hero-band__halo {
    position: absolute;
    top: 0;
    left: 0;
    right: 0;
    bottom: 0;
    align-items: center;
    justify-content: center;
}

/* THE RING IS A PICTURE BECAUSE USS HAS NO `border-style`. There is
 * `border-width` and `border-color` and nothing that takes `dashed`, so the
 * handoff's `stroke-dasharray: 4 12` has no property to land in. This is the
 * same answer `HeroSlot.uss` gives at 96px and NOT the same texture: a
 * stretched ring keeps its dash count and scales its stroke, so hero-ring
 * .png at 264px would draw 30 dashes in 5.5px of ink against this ring's 52
 * in 2px. generate-textures.py's ring block has the arithmetic.
 *
 * NO BORDER AND NO RADIUS ON THIS ELEMENT, deliberately, and for the reason
 * HeroSlot.uss states: a solid ring UNDER the dashed one would show through
 * every gap in the pattern as a continuous line, which is the one thing a
 * dashed ring must not have.
 *
 * TINTED, NOT RECOLOURED. -unity-background-image-tint-color multiplies and
 * the ring is authored white, so white times a tint IS the tint - icons.uss's
 * header has the long form.
 *
 * --violet-pale IS THE HANDOFF'S RING TO WITHIN 4. `Onboarding.dc.html:45`
 * strokes #dcd2f3 and `Splice Reveal:45` / `Hybrid Growth:50` both stroke
 * #d8cdf2; --violet-pale is #d9cdf5, which is 4 from the second and 10 from
 * the first in summed channel distance - inside the spread of the handoff's
 * own three. It is also already a ring tint in this project:
 * `.hero-slot--live .hero-slot__ring` reads it.
 *
 * THIS RING DOES NOT TURN AND THE HANDOFF'S DOES. Every instance carries
 * `class="spin"` - `animation: orbit 20s linear infinite` - and USS on
 * 6000.6.0f1 has `transition-*`, no `@keyframes` and no `animation`. There is
 * no property for it to land in. RECORDED RATHER THAN DROPPED, because a
 * silently vanished declaration is this project's recurring defect
 * (`align-items: baseline`, `text-transform`, `calc()`), and the difference
 * between those and this one is that this is a decision. What was declined:
 * a C# scheduler writing `style.rotate` every frame would produce it, at the
 * cost of a repaint per frame on decoration and - worse - a capture corpus
 * that lands on a different frame every run, which is the one piece of
 * evidence this project has that anything renders at all. HeroSlot made the
 * same call in Task 13 and its ring reads correctly still. */
.hero-band__ring {
    width: var(--band-ring);
    height: var(--band-ring);
    flex-shrink: 0;
    align-items: center;
    justify-content: center;
    background-image: url("project:///Assets/UI/Art/band-ring.png");
    -unity-background-scale-mode: stretch-to-fill;
    -unity-background-image-tint-color: var(--violet-pale);
}

/* THE POOL INSIDE THE RING, AND IT IS 6 FOR 6 WITH IT. Every dashed ring in
 * the bundle has a filled circle behind the subject and none has one
 * without - `Onboarding:46` (r 98), `Splice Reveal:46` (r 106),
 * `Hybrid Growth:51` (r 82), `Splice Chamber:110` (r 46),
 * `Breeder Profile:33` (r 54) and `Gene Lab Event:34` (r 58). They are one
 * ornament, which is why this is a CHILD of the ring and disappears with it.
 *
 * FOUR OF THE SIX ARE A VIOLET POOL AND TWO ARE A WHITE WASH. The last two
 * are not this band: they are the `linear-gradient(160deg, ...)` header cards,
 * whose ring and circle are both `#ffffff` at .8-.85 and .3-.35 opacity
 * because they are drawn ON a coloured panel rather than on white. That is a
 * different surface with a different ornament on it, and it is named here so
 * the count above is not read as six instances of THIS.
 *
 * 196 IS 264 AT THE HANDOFF'S OWN PROPORTION. `Onboarding.dc.html:46` draws
 * it r 98 inside the r 132 ring, and 98 is half of 196 - a square element, so
 * both axes clamp alike and this is a true circle (Theme.uss header item 4).
 * The three band-scale instances sit at 0.74, 0.76 and 0.79 of their ring;
 * this takes the one the band is measured from rather than averaging them.
 * A LITERAL rather than a token because one file reads it, which is this
 * project's standing rule for one-off geometry.
 *
 * --violet-glow IS THE PALETTE OWNER'S RULING, AT THE HANDOFF'S OWN VALUE.
 * The pool first borrowed --violet-pressed (#e7e0f6, 7 from the handoff's
 * #e9e0fb) for lack of a token of its own - flagged as a concern because the
 * name is an interaction state's, not decoration's. The real cost was never
 * the name, it was the coupling: --violet-pressed is `.btn-secondary:active`'s
 * fill (`Theme.uss:383`), so a routine interaction re-tune would otherwise
 * silently move the hero pool on every band screen too, for no reason the
 * two colours share on purpose. The ruling is --violet-glow, the handoff's
 * own value at distance 0 - `Tokens.uss`'s own note beside it has the full
 * measurement history and states the general rule: decoration must not
 * borrow an interaction state's token. --violet-pressed ITSELF DOES NOT
 * CHANGE and keeps its own reader at `Theme.uss:383`.
 *
 * NAMED AFTER AN ANIMATION IT DOES NOT PERFORM. All six handoff pools carry
 * `animation: glow 3.xs ease-in-out infinite` (`Onboarding.dc.html:46`,
 * `Splice Reveal:46`, `Hybrid Growth:51`, `Splice Chamber:110`, `Breeder
 * Profile:33`, `Gene Lab Event:34`) - `glow` is in `Motion.uss`'s recorded-
 * absent list, on the same footing as the ring's `orbit`, so this element
 * is still for the same reason and by the same decision, not a separate
 * drop. */
.hero-band__glow {
    width: 196px;
    height: 196px;
    border-radius: 98px;
    background-color: var(--violet-glow);
    flex-shrink: 0;
}

/* WHAT THE SCREEN PUTS IN THE BAND, above the ring and in normal flow.
 *
 * CENTRED, BECAUSE THAT IS WHAT A BAND IS FOR. Four of the six instances put
 * one subject in the middle of it; the two that stack a padded column
 * (`Splice Chamber:101`, `Hybrid Growth:48`) override `align-items` and
 * `justify-content` from their own sheet, which is the direction a screen's
 * sheet is allowed to reach - downward into its descendants, never upward
 * (StatCell.uss's closing note). Defaulting the other way would leave the
 * centring to be re-derived on every screen that wants the plain form, which
 * is the trade Task 13 spent its brief declining.
 *
 * `flex-grow` SO IT FILLS THE BAND. Without it a centred subject would be
 * centred in its own height rather than in the band's, which is the same
 * nothing this rule looks like it is restating and is not.
 *
 * NOT NAMED `body`. `SectionCard` owns an element named `body` and so does
 * `HeroSlot`, and a founder screen holds all three - so a third element
 * answering that name is a `Q("body")` that means whichever one a future
 * edit happens to create first. `FounderNamingView`'s own `card-title`
 * carries the same note for the same reason. */
.hero-band__subject {
    flex-grow: 1;
    flex-direction: column;
    align-items: center;
    justify-content: center;
}

/* THE SUBJECT GROWS TO FILL THE HALO IT SITS IN, WHEN THERE IS ONE. Phase 9
 * Task 14c fix round 2, new finding 2, measured against the handoff:
 * `Onboarding.dc.html:45` rings at r 132 (264px, --band-ring), `:46` pools
 * at r 98 (196px, this file's own `.hero-band__glow`), `:49-51` draws its
 * subject at 240x240 - 91% of the ring's diameter. A bare `HeroSlot`
 * (`--hero-slot: 96px`, `Tokens.uss`) is 36% of it - the founder reads as a
 * speck inside a large ornament, a measured gap against the handoff and not
 * taste.
 *
 * REACHES INTO `HeroSlot`, A DESCENDANT WHEN ONE IS PLACED IN `Subject`, ON
 * `HeroSlot.uss`'s OWN PRECEDENT: "a component's stylesheet reaches its own
 * descendants ... and nothing else" (StatCell.uss's closing note), and
 * `HeroSlot.uss` already reaches into ITS descendant `CreatureStage` for
 * exactly this reason. `HeroSlot`'s own public shape, and `--hero-slot`
 * itself, DO NOT CHANGE - 96px stays correct where HeroSlot is a list
 * element, in the roster and in Splice Chamber's parent cards. A creature
 * standing alone inside a HeroBand's ring is a different context, and this
 * is that context's own answer.
 *
 * SCOPED TO `--haloed`, NOT TO EVERY `.hero-band__subject`, so a FUTURE
 * HeroSlot inside a RINGLESS band (Splice Chamber's padded column of
 * 74px/112px parent cards, `ring: false`) is untouched - `--haloed` is only
 * added when `ring` is true (`HeroBand.cs`), because USS here has no
 * sibling combinator to key off the halo's presence any other way.
 *
 * 160, NOT 240 OR 196. Made to fit HeroBand's OWN chrome rather than matched
 * to the handoff's literal 240, because the handoff's 240 is the Ark PRISM
 * at onboarding step 1 (`isS1`) - a different asset the founder's band
 * borrows its CHROME from and not its content, and there is no handoff
 * reference for a creature inside this exact ring. 196 (the pool's own
 * size) was rejected: a disc that size would sit exactly on the pool's edge
 * and hide it completely, wasting the palette owner's `--violet-glow`
 * ruling two rules above. 160 leaves 18px of the pool visible on each side
 * - a clear violet halo behind the creature - and is still comfortably
 * inside `vetch.png`'s own 192x192 native resolution
 * (`client/Assets/UI/Resources/Art/creatures/bodies/`), so this reads
 * closer to the art's native size than the old 80px disc did, not a blur.
 *
 * `.hero-slot__ring` IS HIDDEN HERE, NOT STRETCHED. HeroSlot.uss's own ring
 * is `hero-ring.png`, 30 dashes at a 2px stroke authored for a 96px slot
 * (`generate-textures.py:268-272`); stretched to 160px the same way that
 * script's own comment measured stretching it to the band's 264px
 * (`generate-textures.py:176-180`: "stroke 2px -> 2 * 264/96 = 5.5px"), the
 * stroke comes out 2 x 160/96 = 3.3px - heavier than authored, and a
 * second dashed ring only 52px inside `--band-ring`'s own radius (132 - 80)
 * besides - close enough to compete with it rather than read as a separate
 * detail. HeroBand's own ring already frames the subject; HeroSlot's does
 * not need to draw a second one here. */
.hero-band__subject--haloed .hero-slot {
    width: 160px;
    height: 160px;
}
.hero-band__subject--haloed .hero-slot__ring {
    display: none;
}
.hero-band__subject--haloed .hero-slot__disc {
    width: 160px;
    height: 160px;
    border-radius: 80px;
}
/* HeroSlot's default live stage is 80px for a compact slot. In a hero band
 * that leaves the render at half the size of the 160px subject. Keep the
 * portrait and its slot in the same frame. */
.hero-band__subject--haloed .hero-slot .creature-stage__frame {
    width: 160px;
    height: 160px;
    border-radius: 80px;
}

/* THE DEEP BAND - Phase 10 Task 1.6, the approved mockups' hero framing:
 * the creature on slate with a brass ring and a violet glow, every label
 * inside it in paper ink. No ramp texture: the surface is one deep fill. */
.hero-band--deep .hero-band__surface {
    background-image: none;
    background-color: var(--surface-deep);
}
.hero-band--deep .hero-band__ring { -unity-background-image-tint-color: var(--brass); }
.hero-band--deep .hero-band__glow { background-color: var(--hud-frame-edge); }
.hero-band--deep Label,
.hero-band--deep .t-hero,
.hero-band--deep .t-section,
.hero-band--deep .t-secondary,
.hero-band--deep .t-micro { color: var(--surface-paper); }
/* A tinted pill inside the deep band keeps its own ink: the reveal's amber
 * "mutation rolled" pill read paper-on-amber on the simulator walk. */
.hero-band--deep .panel-amber Label,
.hero-band--deep .t-warn { color: var(--amber-text); }
